---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro';
import { findInsight } from '../../insights';

const insight = findInsight('closing-the-books-in-five-days')!;
---

<Article
  insight={insight}
  standfirst="A close is slow for structural reasons, not lazy ones. What to fix first when month-end runs long, in the order that actually compounds."
>
  <p>
    When a month-end close takes five weeks, the instinct is to look for
    slowness — someone working through a queue too gently, a step that could be
    done faster. That is almost never what is happening. A slow close is usually
    a close with too many dependencies, and dependencies do not respond to
    effort.
  </p>

  <p>
    Here is the order we would fix things in, based on what tends to compound.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Stop waiting for information you could already have</h2>

  <p>
    The single largest component of a long close is usually waiting: for bank
    statements, for a supplier invoice, for a schedule from an operational team
    that does not know the close is blocked on them.
  </p>

  <p>
    Some of that waiting is unavoidable. Most of it is avoidable by making the
    request earlier and making its status visible. A request list where both
    sides can see what is outstanding removes an entire category of delay — the
    delay caused by nobody realising they were the blocker.
  </p>

  <p>
    Measure this before you fix anything else. Take your last three closes and
    mark, for each day, whether work was happening or whether the close was
    waiting. If waiting is more than half, nothing downstream matters yet.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Move reconciliation off the critical path</h2>

  <p>
    Bank and control account reconciliation is often treated as a month-end
    task. It does not need to be. Transactions arrive continuously, so they can
    be classified and matched continuously, with only the exceptions left for
    the close.
  </p>

  <p>
    The shift here is from "reconcile at month end" to "keep reconciled, resolve
    exceptions at month end". The work is the same in total but almost none of
    it sits between you and the close. In practice this is the change that moves
    a five-week close to a two-week close.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Decide what actually has to be right before you report</h2>

  <p>
    Not every balance carries the same weight. A close often runs long because
    every account is treated with equal care, including several that could not
    move the reported result if they tried.
  </p>

  <p>
    Set materiality by account, in writing, and agree it with whoever signs. Then
    accept that some accounts get a light review and some get a full one. This
    feels like a compromise on quality; it is the opposite. Attention is finite,
    and spreading it evenly means the accounts that matter get less of it than
    they should.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Kill the second version of the truth</h2>

  <p>
    Almost every slow close has a workbook in it — a spreadsheet that takes the
    ledger and turns it into the reported numbers through steps that exist
    nowhere else.
  </p>

  <p>
    The workbook is not the problem in itself. The problem is that it constitutes
    a second version of the accounts, and the two have to be reconciled by hand
    every period. Worse, the mapping inside it is usually different from the
    mapping used for the statutory statements and different again from the one
    used for tax, so three answers exist for the same question.
  </p>

  <p>
    Map the chart of accounts once, in one place, in a way that can carry
    statutory presentation and tax classification simultaneously. Derive
    everything else from it. This removes the reconciliation entirely rather than
    making it faster.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Only then, look at sequencing</h2>

  <p>
    Once you are not waiting, not reconciling on the critical path, not
    over-reviewing immaterial accounts, and not maintaining a parallel set of
    books — then it is worth looking at the order of tasks and what can run in
    parallel.
  </p>

  <p>
    Doing this step first is the common mistake. Re-sequencing a process whose
    real constraint is a missing bank statement produces a beautifully
    sequenced process that still takes five weeks.
  </p>

  <h2>What five days actually requires</h2>

  <p>
    A five-day close is not heroics. It is a close where:
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>Nothing is requested for the first time after the period ends.</li>
    <li>Reconciliations are current on day one, with only exceptions open.</li>
    <li>
      Review effort is allocated by materiality that somebody has agreed in
      advance.
    </li>
    <li>
      The reported figures are derived from the ledger rather than rebuilt
      beside it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every figure can be traced to source without asking the person who
      prepared it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    That last one is what makes the speed sustainable rather than fragile. A fast
    close that depends on one person's knowledge is not fast — it is borrowed
    against a future month when that person is unavailable.
  </p>
</Article>
